flash-runner @VERSION@

What this package installed
  @CRX@
      the extension, signed with the project key; its ID is @ID@
  <browser>/extensions/@ID@.json
      tells the browser to install that file: Chrome (/opt/google/chrome/extensions and /usr/share/google-chrome/extensions),
      Edge (/usr/share/microsoft-edge/extensions) and Chromium (/usr/share/chromium/extensions)
  /usr/lib/flash-helper/flash-helper
      the online game helper, which gives games the raw TCP sockets a browser cannot open; it is registered
      system-wide for Chrome, Chromium, Edge, Brave and Firefox (/etc/.../native-messaging-hosts/com.flashrunner.sockets.json,
      /usr/lib/mozilla/native-messaging-hosts for Firefox) and only this extension's ID may talk to it

Using it
  Restart the browser. The extension shows up in chrome://extensions (edge://extensions) by itself; on Linux the browser does
  not ask first. The extension's settings page then shows "Online games: Installed".
  Brave and Vivaldi have no documented directory for this, so the extension is not added there automatically.

Updating
  Install the newer package. The JSON carries the new version, and the browser updates the extension the next time it starts.

Removing
  sudo apt remove flash-runner
  The JSON files go with it, and the browser removes the extension at its next start.
  If you remove the extension yourself in chrome://extensions, the browser remembers that and does not install it again.
  The standalone helper (flash-helper_amd64.deb) installs the same helper without the extension.
